## Reading with a notepad

Attention lets every token look back at every earlier token (<ChapterRef to="attention" />). A <GlossaryPopover term="recurrent-model">recurrent model</GlossaryPopover>
works differently: it reads one token at a time and keeps a small, fixed-size **memory**, a vector it
updates after every token. To predict the next token it has only that memory, never the earlier
tokens themselves. It's like reading a book while keeping notes on a single index card.

Networks like this are much older than transformers.<Cite id="elman1990" /> Below is a tiny one,
trained in your browser on the rhymes from <ChapterRef to="predicting-by-counting" />, like the model in <ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" />. Train it, then slide through
the phrase and watch its 32-number memory change with each character:

<RecurrentMemory />

Before training, the memory changes but means nothing, and the guesses are random. After training,
it has learned to store what matters for the next character. After "humpty d" it expects a *u*:
all it has is its memory, and the memory still holds enough of "humpty" to know "dumpty" comes
next.

## Fixed memory, growing text

The appeal is cost. A transformer's memory grows with the text, because it keeps something for
every token so later tokens can look back: the KV cache from <ChapterRef to="caching" />. A recurrent model's memory
stays the same size however long the text gets:

<MemoryGrowth />

The catch is the index card. Everything the model will ever need must be squeezed into those few
numbers as it reads, and whatever it doesn't note down is gone for good. Early recurrent networks
were bad at keeping information for long; the LSTM added *gates* that learn what to keep and what
to forget.<Cite id="hochreiter1997" /> For years, LSTMs were the standard way to model language,
until the transformer, which needs no recurrence at all, replaced them.<Cite id="vaswani2017" />

## The idea comes back

Recurrent ideas are back in newer designs called *state-space models*. Mamba, from 2023, updates
its fixed-size state in a way that depends on the token it is reading, so it can choose what to
keep and what to forget, and its cost grows only in step with the length of the text.<Cite id="gu2023" />
The fixed memory still has a price: a transformer can copy a long passage it was shown word for
word, while a model with a fixed-size state struggles once the passage outgrows its
memory.<Cite id="jelassi2024" />

<RecapCard>

- A recurrent model reads one token at a time and carries a fixed-size memory forward.
- Its memory doesn't grow with the text, so long texts are cheap, but it has to decide what to
  forget.
- Attention keeps every earlier token around, which costs more but lets the model look anything up
  again.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** everything so far has read text. How can a model that reads tokens look at a
picture?
